/** @mainpage USBRadioPlus developer reference

USBRadioPlus implements the RadioPlus Asterisk channel technology, native radio
signaling, source processing, and the interactive tuning utility. This reference
describes implementation contracts. The installed manual pages describe radio
configuration and operation.

@section architecture Source layout

- chan_usbradioplus.c adapts OSS/libusb radio devices to Asterisk.
- chan_usbradioplus_modern.c adapts the ASL radio-device API and PortAudio.
- usbradioplus_channel_common.h defines shared channel lifecycle and tuning calls.
- usbradioplus_channel_core.h defines PCM routing, queues, clock recovery, and echo state.
- usbradioplus_native_tick.c runs the native receive/transmit block sequence.
- usbradioplus_processing.h exposes validated profiles and live processing controls.
- txagc_config describes the shared FFmpeg graph. Filtering, emphasis,
  equalization, dynamics, and limiting are implemented by that graph.
- usbradioplus_radio.h contains carrier, CTCSS, measurement, and transmit-signaling state.
- usbradioplus_ctcss.h and usbradioplus_hardware.h provide tone generation and
  parallel-port radio programming.
- scripts/usbradioplus-tune implements the screen-reader-friendly tuner.
- tests and tests_py contain executable unit, failure-injection, integration,
  configuration, utility, and install checks. Upstream reference fixtures are test data.

@section units Audio buffers and units

The CM119 path runs at 48,000 samples per second with 960 samples in a 20 ms
mono block. App_rpt starts at 8,000 samples per second; each channel stores its
rate explicitly. Equal input and output rates bypass sample-rate conversion.
Signed PCM spans -32768 through 32767. Native floating-point workspaces retain
PCM amplitude units. FFmpeg format conversion supplies the graph's normalized
audio representation. dBFS measurements use the full signed PCM range.

The app_rpt-to-CM119 elastic FIFO starts with a 120 ms occupancy target. Keyed
underruns raise it by 10 ms, up to 170 ms; three minutes of stable keyed blocks
accumulated across transmissions lower it by 10 ms, but never below 110 ms.
Both queues hold 200 ms so startup padding and a complete resampled frame fit
at the maximum target. These are occupancy targets, not an end-to-end latency
guarantee: each device interval consumes 20 ms, and network jitter buffering
adds its own delay.

Both adapters admit app_rpt program frames continuously, including idle
silence, except while echo playback owns the input. PTT transitions preserve
the FIFO, resampler state, clock recovery, and concealment history. Only
stream startup or recovery seeds the FIFO reserve. Resampled streams receive
one additional 20 ms leading silence frame so the sinc converter can fill
its history and reach the FIFO target. If the program queue empties and
fewer than 20 ms of native audio remain, one silence frame releases the
converter's retained audio before underrun recovery. Further
padding requires another queued input frame; a continuously arriving stream
does not receive this tail padding while its FIFO reserve lasts. Native-rate
input bypasses these resampling steps. Padding affects PCM audio only: app_rpt
and tuning retain ownership of PTT, and FIFO draining does not extend it.

The device writer submits one 48 kHz stereo block per completed capture
interval, including while idle. Transmit activity selects the rendered block
or an immutable silence block; COS and CTCSS decode do not control device
write cadence. The OSS writer does not prepend silence when its queue empties.
Its queue-depth bound and short/failed-write diagnostics still apply during
idle operation. PortAudio retains the ASL audio API's device recovery.

When the local chain is enabled, its enabled RNNoise and FFmpeg dynamics
stages advance every native interval with hardware_rx_cpu_saver_enabled=no.
RX CPU saver bypasses those stages while the receiver is unqualified. Existing
receive qualification, repeat routing, and final transmit muting still
control audibility; processing continuity does not key the transmitter.

Hardware mixer settings use 0 through 999. Hardware gain is expressed in dB
relative to setting 500. Receiver CTCSS decoder gain affects the detector input;
it does not change voice gain. Native CTCSS is mixed after transmitter dynamics
and limiting. Saturating PCM conversion prevents signed wraparound.

@section concurrency Thread and resource ownership

Each channel owns its native graphs, resamplers, detector, queues, and echo
recording. The app_rpt queue mutex protects frames passed from Asterisk to the
device worker. The processing settings mutex protects profile snapshots.
Never hold the processing settings mutex while looking up or locking an
Asterisk channel: app_rpt can read processing settings while holding channel
locks. The scanner copies the needed profile data before channel lookup.

An incoming link audiohook owns its graph through an Asterisk datastore.
Stopping the processing subsystem joins the scanner and detaches those hooks
before releasing its state. Hardware adapters own their device references and
coordinate audio/HID workers when swapping USB assignments.

@section verification Building and checking the reference

Run `make docs` with Doxygen 1.9.8 or newer, Graphviz, and Python 3 installed.
Generated HTML is written to `build/doxygen/html`; XML is written beside it. Doxygen checks
undocumented symbols and enum values, parameter and return documentation, and
invalid markup. Every diagnostic fails the target.

The required quality workflow runs Doxygen concurrently with lint and static
analysis. All three results must succeed before the Debian 12/13 amd64/arm64
test matrix. Release callers depend on that same required quality workflow.
Only successful main-branch checks publish this reference to GitHub Pages.

Shell documentation uses double-hash comments. The documentation filter exposes
actual shell function names and those comments to Doxygen without executing
shell code or supplying missing documentation. Python uses standard docstrings
with Doxygen commands for parameters and return values.
*/
